                         Good Practises

>> separate catch blocks for different Exception
>> if having parent + child exception | child exception will get chance
   order of catch block
   *** compile time error :- exception already caught
   child1 >> child2 >> parent -  Valid
   parent >> child1 >> child2 - IN Valid  | exception already caught
>>   try{
         stmt 1
         stmt 2 // try block with multiple catch | possible and recommended to use
       }
    catch(E e1){
      }
    catch(D e2){
      }

*** MISSING >> Multi-catch block (Java 7+, still standard practice in 17) - good practice addition
    >> if 2 or more exceptions need SAME handling code, combine into one catch using |
       try{
           stmt 1
           stmt 2
       }
       catch(E1 | E2 e){       // single catch, handles either E1 or E2 identically
           // common handling code
       }
    >> RULE - E1 and E2 must NOT have parent-child relationship (compile time error if related)
    >> the exception reference (e) is implicitly final - cannot be reassigned inside catch block

*** MISSING >> try-with-resources (Java 7+, enhanced in Java 9) - good practice for resource-related exceptions
    Before Java 9:
       try(FileReader fr = new FileReader("a.txt")){
           // use fr
       }
       // fr auto-closed, AutoCloseable.close() called automatically - no explicit finally needed
    Java 9+ enhancement:
       FileReader fr = new FileReader("a.txt");
       try(fr){                     // can use an already-declared effectively-final variable directly
           // use fr
       }
    >> resource must implement AutoCloseable/Closeable
    >> multiple resources closed in REVERSE order of declaration
    >> suppressed exceptions (from close()) attached via getSuppressed() if try block also throws

*** MISSING >> Pattern Matching instanceof (Java 16+, standard in 17) - avoids ClassCastException proactively
    Before:
       if(obj instanceof String){
           String s = (String) obj;   // manual cast - risk of ClassCastException if skipped/misused
       }
    Java 17:
       if(obj instanceof String s){   // safe - no explicit cast, no ClassCastException risk
           System.out.println(s.length());
       }
    >> directly relevant to Common Exceptions #3 (ClassCastException) below - reduces the scenarios
       where that exception can even occur due to careless manual casting

