*** DEPRECATED >> Related to exception/error handling in Java 17

    1. SecurityManager and related security exceptions - DEPRECATED FOR REMOVAL in Java 17 (JEP 411)
       >> SecurityManager.checkXXX() methods used to throw SecurityException as part of
          the old sandboxing model (applets, restricted permissions)
       >> the ENTIRE SecurityManager mechanism is deprecated for removal - relevant here because
          SecurityException-based exception handling code tied to SecurityManager is now legacy
       >> use instead >> OS-level sandboxing, containers, or module system (JPMS) restrictions

    2. Thread.stop(Throwable obj) - DEPRECATED FOR REMOVAL (was already deprecated pre-9, reinforced since)
       >> used to forcibly throw an arbitrary Throwable in the target thread, causing
          unpredictable/unsafe termination (ThreadDeath error internally)
       >> use instead >> cooperative cancellation - a volatile boolean flag or
          Thread.interrupt() + checking Thread.isInterrupted() inside the run loop

    3. Runtime.runFinalizersOnExit() / System.runFinalizersOnExit() - already removed pre-17, still
       relevant to mention since it interacted with finalize()-related ExceptionInInitializerError-style
       issues (#6 above) >> avoid designs depending on finalizers altogether;
       use try-with-resources / java.lang.ref.Cleaner instead